# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the host-side harness.
#
# Everything here is either an artefact of the stub device or a pre-existing
# condition in code the compression work did not touch. Nothing in the block
# index, the extent allocator or the codec paths is suppressed - those must stay
# clean, and they are.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The stub device makes an intentional race visible.
#
# A reader snapshots a block's index entry, then DMAs from the slot it names.
# If a writer replaces that block in the meantime, the slot can be freed and
# handed to a different block, so the reader's copy may run concurrently with
# another worker's write to those same bytes. That is by design: the read is
# validated against the entry's generation before anything is decompressed, and
# a mismatch discards the bytes and retries. The stale copy is harmless because
# grain and length come from an entry that was valid when published, so it is
# always inside the heap.
#
# On real hardware these are DMA engine transfers and never a C-level data race
# at all. The stub implements them as plain memcpy on host memory, which is what
# puts them in front of ThreadSanitizer. Suppressed at the stub boundary so a
# genuine race anywhere else in the copy path would still be reported.
race:cuMemcpyHtoDAsync_v2
race:cuMemcpyDtoHAsync_v2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pre-existing: the SIGTERM drain path (nbd-vram.c:700-725, added for issue #19)
# communicates between the signal handler and the workers through `volatile int`
# rather than atomics - g_running, g_term_requested and g_client_fds[]. Benign in
# practice for single-word flags, and unchanged by the compression work, so it is
# out of scope here rather than fixed under a test-harness commit. Worth cleaning
# up separately with __atomic accessors.
race:sig_handler
race:clients_connected
